# UI Evidence — pixels, not diffs

Origin: adapted from a protocol of the reference donor-harness (UI shipped without being
seen, broken capture toolchain, hand-pasted prints as the sole visual-regression
test).

## 0. Prerequisite: install the engine (run once, not included automatically)

Copier materializes the ENGINE (the files below) in
`{{ harness_web_app_dir }}/`, but does **not** edit your app's `package.json`
— that's target-project content, outside what the harness templates. Before
§1 works, confirm the steps below:

1. **Engine files** (generated by `use_ui_evidence: true`, rescue plan
   §R3) — must exist in `{{ harness_web_app_dir }}/`:
   - `scripts/ui-evidence.sh` — orchestrator (preflight + capture + manifest).
   - `scripts/gen-visual-routes.mjs` — SKELETON generator for `routes.json`;
     fill in the TODO with your project's real routes (or edit
     `tests/visual/routes.json` directly — see `routes.json.example`).
   - `playwright.visual.config.ts` — dedicated Playwright config (viewports,
     `setup` project, `maxDiffPixelRatio`).
   - `tests/visual/evidence.spec.ts` — capture (route × theme).
   - `tests/visual/baseline.spec.ts` — regression (`toHaveScreenshot`).
   - `tests/visual/theme-map.config.ts` — **customize here** this project's real
     themes (the default is a single `default` theme, with no manipulation of
     class/attribute/localStorage — generic, unaware of your design system).
   - `tests/visual/auth.setup.ts` — **STUB**: throws an error until you implement
     `loginForVisualEvidence(page)` (see the TODO in the file), or delete the
     file if the captured routes are 100% public.
   - `tests/visual/routes.json` — minimal seed (`[{"name":"home","path":"/"}]`).

   If any of these files does NOT exist, the engine wasn't installed
   (`use_ui_evidence` was `false` on `copier copy`, or this project
   predates R3) — run `copier update` or install manually before
   proceeding; the Stop hook `ui-evidence-gate` stays INACTIVE (fail-open) until
   it exists.

2. **`package.json` wiring** (the harness does NOT edit this — add it by hand,
   once, in `{{ harness_web_app_dir }}/package.json` → `"scripts"`):

   ```json
   "ui:evidence": "bash scripts/ui-evidence.sh"
   ```

   Without that entry, the `ui-evidence-gate` hook detects "command not wired" and
   does not block (fail-open) — the presence of `scripts/ui-evidence.sh` on disk
   ALONE does not count: the gate requires the `"ui:evidence"` script inside the
   `package.json` (it's the only proof the command actually runs). Quick
   self-check: `grep -q '"ui:evidence"' {{ harness_web_app_dir }}/package.json`.

3. **`@playwright/test` dependency** — the engine files (`playwright.visual.config.ts`,
   `tests/visual/*.spec.ts`) import `@playwright/test`; Copier does not install
   npm packages. Add it as a devDependency (`npm install -D @playwright/test`)
   and install the browser: `npx playwright install chromium` (see "Chromium" in §3 below).

## 1. Canonical command

```bash
cd {{ harness_web_app_dir }} && npm run ui:evidence -- <label> [--routes /a,/b] [--themes <csv from tests/visual/theme-map.config.ts>] [--project desktop|mobile-md|tablet]
```

- `<label>`: `before` (BEFORE touching) | `after` (afterward) | free slug.
- Without `--routes`: all routes from `tests/visual/routes.json`.
- Without `--themes`: ALL themes declared in `tests/visual/theme-map.config.ts`.
- Output: `{{ harness_evidence_dir }}/<label>/` → `<route>__<theme>__<project>.png` +
  `.meta.json` (HTTP status + console errors) + `manifest.json` (sha256,
  git HEAD, timestamp) — the manifest is what the Stop hook looks for.

## 2. before/after protocol (intentional visual change)

1. BEFORE editing: `npm run ui:evidence -- before --routes <affected>`.
2. Edit.
3. `npm run ui:evidence -- after --routes <affected>`.
4. LOOK at the PNGs (Read) — before vs after, in ALL themes. Confirm:
   the intended change happened; nothing else changed; `consoleErrors` did not grow.
5. Only then report, linking the PNG paths in the verdict.

## 3. Environment facts (most historical failures come from here)

- **Dev target = http://localhost:{{ harness_dev_web_port }}.**{% if harness_reserved_ports %} Port {{ harness_reserved_ports }} may be
  occupied by another local tool — the script's preflight detects it and aborts.{% endif %}
  {%- if project_name %} Bring the stack up with the `run-{{ project_name }}` skill if the preflight fails.{% endif %}
- **Auth:** `tests/visual/auth.setup.ts` is a **STUB** (§0) — throws an error until
  you implement `loginForVisualEvidence(page)` for this project, or
  delete the file if the captured routes are 100% public. It assumes
  no pre-existing e2e helper.
- **Themes:** the real matrix lives in `tests/visual/theme-map.config.ts` — the
  factory default is a single `default` theme with no manipulation of
  class/attribute/localStorage. Customize it there (brand via attribute, light/dark
  mode via the `.dark` class, localStorage seed pre-hydration —
  whatever this project uses). `HARNESS_UI_EVIDENCE_THEMES` ({{ harness_ui_evidence_themes }})
  is only the default hint documented here — what actually decides the capturable
  themes is `theme-map.config.ts`.
- **Chromium:** binaries in `~/.cache/ms-playwright/chromium-*`; if the
  preflight reports it missing: `cd {{ harness_web_app_dir }} && npx playwright install chromium`.

## 4. MCP Playwright/chrome-devtools vs this script

MCP = INTERACTIVE exploration and debug (clicking, inspecting, live flow).
FINAL delivery evidence = ALWAYS this script (deterministic, with manifest
and console attached; MCP snapshots go stale and leave no auditable proof).

## 5. Stop hook ui-evidence-gate

Blocks end of turn with `{{ harness_web_app_dir }}/{app,components,styles}/*.tsx|css`
changed without a manifest newer than the change. Legitimate escapes:
- run the canonical command (§1);
- provably non-visual change or stack down:
  `touch {{ harness_evidence_dir }}/SKIP` (valid for {{ harness_ui_evidence_skip_ttl_seconds // 3600 }}h) and DECLARE the reason in the verdict.
Fabricating SKIP to skip visual validation of a visual change = violation.

## 6. Regression assertions (complementary)

`tests/visual/baseline.spec.ts` (toHaveScreenshot with committed baselines)
remains the REGRESSION test. This skill produces review EVIDENCE.
Don't confuse them: baseline fails the build; evidence informs the human/agent.

## 7. Contrast PAIR evaluator{% if use_ds_gate %} — `.harness/lib/ds-pair-eval.py`{% endif %}

{% if use_ds_gate -%}
Installed by `use_ds_gate` (enabled in this project): when tokenizing, the
classic error is swapping the TEXT color without checking the BACKGROUND — `text-text` over
fixed `bg-white` ends up white-on-white in dark; `text-white` over
`bg-muted` (neutral token) disappears in light. A function like this judges a pair
deterministically across the project's themes (`PROJECT_THEMES` from
`.harness/harness.conf`), without looking at a pixel.

```bash
python3 .harness/lib/ds-pair-eval.py --text text-text --bg bg-white           # → FAIL in dark
python3 .harness/lib/ds-pair-eval.py --text text-primary-text --bg bg-primary # → PASS (brand button)
python3 .harness/lib/ds-pair-eval.py --text text-muted-text --bg bg-card --non-text
echo '[{"text":"text-white","bg":"bg-muted"}]' | python3 .harness/lib/ds-pair-eval.py --batch  # JSON batch
```

Resolves globals.css tokens per theme (with cascade inheritance) + raw Tailwind
palette (slate/zinc/neutral/gray/stone/white/black); composes opacity (`/70`). Prints
ratio+PASS/FAIL per theme and the "fits / does NOT fit + failing themes"
verdict. Exit 1 if it fails in any theme (usable in a gate and by audit subagents).

**When a pair FITS:** the text contrasts ≥4.5:1 (text) / ≥3:1 (non-text)
with the REAL background in ALL themes. Real background = go up the DOM to the surface that
paints behind. Valid pair = text and background on the SAME axis (both neutral tokens that
adapt together, or both fixed and paired). Mixing token-text + fixed-background (or
vice versa) is what breaks — that's what this function catches.
{%- else -%}
Not installed in this project (`use_ds_gate: false`). If this project has an
equivalent contrast evaluator, it solves the same problem: judge
deterministically whether a text×background pair fits (≥4.5:1 text / ≥3:1
non-text) in ALL of the project's themes without relying on looking at a pixel — the
classic error is swapping the TEXT color without checking the BACKGROUND (`text-text` over
fixed `bg-white` becomes white-on-white in dark).
{%- endif %}
